//? Title: Test marginal terms in an unbalanced linear model
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare Type II and Type III term tests

Three methods are run under cool and warm conditions, with unequal
replication and known relative precision. The linear model includes
the interaction between method and batch condition.

The Type II test for method adjusts for batch condition and leaves the
interaction out of both models being compared. The Type III test with
sum coding compares method means averaged equally over the two batch
conditions. With treatment coding, the Type III method test compares
methods at the reference condition, Cool. All tests use the residual
mean square from the same full model.

```{cpp, id=marginal-anova, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/lm.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using datamunge::dstruct::DataFrame;
    using datamunge::stats::FactorContrast;
    using datamunge::stats::LM;
    using datamunge::stats::MarginalAnovaType;

    DataFrame data;
    data.add_column("method", std::vector<std::string>{
        "A", "A", "A", "A", "A", "B", "B", "B", "B", "B", "C", "C", "C", "C", "C"});
    data.add_column("batch", std::vector<std::string>{
        "Cool", "Cool", "Cool", "Warm", "Warm", "Cool", "Cool", "Warm",
        "Warm", "Warm", "Cool", "Cool", "Warm", "Warm", "Warm"});
    data.add_column("yield", std::vector<double>{8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 12, 14, 21, 22, 24, 13, 15, 12, 14, 14});
    data.add_column("precision", std::vector<double>{1, 2, 1, 1, 3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2, 1, 3});

    const LM sum(data, "yield ~ method * batch", {
        .weights_column = "precision",
        .contrasts = {{"method", FactorContrast::Sum}, {"batch", FactorContrast::Sum}},
    });
    const LM treatment(data, "yield ~ method * batch", {.weights_column = "precision"});
    const auto type_ii = sum.marginal_anova(MarginalAnovaType::II);
    const auto type_iii = sum.marginal_anova(MarginalAnovaType::III);
    const auto reference = treatment.marginal_anova(MarginalAnovaType::III);

    std::println("| Term | Numerator df | Type II F | Type III F, sum | Type III F, treatment |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i + 1 < type_ii.size(); ++i)
        std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", type_ii[i].term,
                     type_ii[i].degrees_of_freedom, type_ii[i].f_value,
                     type_iii[i].f_value, reference[i].f_value);
    std::println("\nThe full model has {} residual degrees of freedom.", type_ii.back().degrees_of_freedom);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Term | Numerator df | Type II F | Type III F, sum | Type III F, treatment |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| method | 2 | 46.910 | 39.648 | 13.071 |
| batch | 1 | 41.689 | 47.594 | 13.933 |
| method:batch | 2 | 25.616 | 25.616 | 25.616 |

The full model has 9 residual degrees of freedom.
// result_end

The interaction has no containing term in this model, so all three
columns test the same interaction. The main-effect tests answer
different questions because the effect of one factor depends on the
level of the other.

[Fox and Weisberg, car Anova reference|https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/car/refman/car.html#Anova]
